<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">École William Ponty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1903</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as École normale William Ponty, William Ponty School</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The École <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-merlaud-ponty">William Ponty</a> was a teacher-training college in French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>, founded at Saint-Louis in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a> in 1903, moved to Gorée in 1913 and later to Sébikotane. Named after the governor-general William Merlaud-Ponty, it trained African teachers, clerks and medical assistants for the whole federation. Its graduates included many leaders of independence, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modibo-keita">Modibo Keïta</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamani-diori">Hamani Diori</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/felix-houphouet-boigny">Félix Houphouët-Boigny</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ecole-william-ponty.jpg" alt="École William Ponty" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: HaguardDuNord (talk) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gor%C3%A9e_-_Ancienne_%C3%A9cole_normale_William_Ponty.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
